## *Tentacles of Progress* ##

### Chapter 1 ###

#### Technology and Western-Tropical Relations ####

(4) "Despite a century of European rule, India had not become industrialized as Marx had expected, nor had any other non-Western country except Japan. Yet they were not untouched by the upheavals of that century. Theirs was no simple delay in some inevitable process of industrialization, but another, less welcome evolution: the transformation of traditional economies into modern underdeveloped ones."

(4) "For Asia and Africa, however, 1914 was the middle of the colonial era."

- i.e. not a turning point as it was in europe and north america

(4) "What these events have in common is not only the fact of Western intrusion, but the technical innovations which gave Europeans the power to intrude."

- technology intertwined with power

Five interactions between technological change and European imperialism

1. (5) "penetration of Asia and Africa by Europeans and the conquest of colonial powers." 1870ish. Successful because of a 'sudden shift in technology' - such as steamboats, firearms, quinine, railways, telegraphs, etc 
2. (6) "an expanding Western economy on world trade. In the nineteenth century, the industrialization of the Western nations stimulated a growing demand for the products of the tropics"
3. (6) "Once in control of an area, Western colonialists were not content to administer their new subjects and tax the existing economy, as previous conquerors had done. Instead, they strove to increase production and lower the cost of tropical products by applying Western industrial and scientific methods. Thus our third interaction is a massive transfer of technology from the West to Africa and Asia."
	- This is related to the second point - tropical goods needed to be cheap if they were to be marketed in mass to the public
	- (7) "The transfer did not, however, diversify the tropical economies, nor did it significantly raise per capita incomes. In other words, the tropical economies grew, but did not develop."
4. Cultural consequence: (7) "Colonized people were not mere objects at the hands of the colonizers. The invasion of their countries by a technologically more advanced culture awakened not only the well-known movements for national independence, but also a desire to obtain more Western products and share in the benefits of Western technology."
	- (7) "In the twentieth century, motor vehicles, televisions, and modern weapons have become irresistible but barely affordable temptations for the peoples of poor countries."
5. The changes stimulated by the west in poorer countries were developed for the benefit of the west. Products procured from the tropics were abandoned if cheaper substitutes were found: (8) "In other words, the industrial nations were becoming less and less dependent on world trade, while the tropical countries had little or no industry to fall back on when trade faltered."

On those left behind (a quote from Natahn Keyfitz): (9) "They are worse than exploited - they are irrelevant. The exploited could always strike against those who were making a profit on them; what recourse is there for the unexploitable, locked out of the market by the shift of prices?"

#### The Transfer Process ####

(9) "The transfer of technology from one society to another, and especially from one civilization to another, is of an altogether higher order of complexity, and no theory has yet emerged to encompass it all. Yet certain basic premises are clear. First of all, the transfer of technology is not one process but two. One of these is the relocation, from one area to another, of equipment and methods, along with the experts to operate them. The other is the diffusion from one society to another of the knowledge, skills, and attitudes related to a particular device or process. Thus we will distinguish the geographic relocation from the cultural diffusion of technology."

